{
  "title": "Disjoint Mask Pairs",
  "description": "## Task\n\nEach device has a permission mask, an integer whose set bits are the permissions it holds. Two devices can share a channel when they hold no permission in common, meaning the bitwise AND of their masks is `0`. Count the pairs of devices `(i, j)` with `i < j` that can share a channel.\n\n## Input\n\n- `masks` — the mask of each device.\n\n## Output\n\nThe number of index pairs `i < j` with `masks[i] & masks[j] == 0`.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\ndisjoint_mask_pairs([1, 2, 3])  # returns 2\ndisjoint_mask_pairs([0, 0])  # returns 1\ndisjoint_mask_pairs([7])  # returns 0\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 0 ≤ length of `masks` ≤ 200000\n- 0 ≤ each mask < 2^17\n- Comparing every pair is too slow for the longest lists.\n",
  "signature": "def disjoint_mask_pairs(masks: list[int]) -> int",
  "topic": "subset sum over masks",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "category": "math-bits",
